Derive sampled feedback separately from numerical time stepping (@engineering-feedback-sampling: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../../utilities/water-and-sanitation/operations/A-tank-balance-explains-control-offset-delay-and-saturation.lingua#r_2R6H6TT78N3TG1PYZMNFJ14RYB") { r_00B508106805C3B41D358D4BF7
Now change the controller's physical timing contract. At t_j=jh, measure θ_j+b, compute u_j=u₀+K(r−θ_j−b), and hold that input constant for the whole interval of duration h>0. Assume instantaneous sampling/update, no quantization or extra delay, constant disturbance and the same continuous plant. This is an ideal sampled controller. Solving the open-loop plant exactly between updates gives, for G>0 and a=exp(−Gh/C),

θ_(j+1)=aθ_j+(1−a)(u_j+d)/G=ρθ_j+β,

where ρ=a−(K/G)(1−a) and β=(1−a)[u₀+K(r−b)+d]/G. The fixed point equals the unlimited continuous equilibrium, but deviations follow z_(j+1)=ρz_j. Therefore asymptotic stability is exactly |ρ|<1. Negative ρ alternates the sign of the sampled deviation; |ρ|≥1 does not decay. With G=0, direct integration gives ρ=1−hK/C and β=h[u₀+K(r−b)+d]/C.

For G>0,K≥0 and any finite h>0, ρ<1. If K≤G, then ρ>−1 as well. If K>G, the remaining condition is exp(−Gh/C)>(K−G)/(K+G), or h<(C/G)ln[(K+G)/(K−G)]. At equality ρ=−1 and deviations alternate without decay. These are exact results for the stipulated hold behavior, not general sampling rules for all plants.

For C=12,G=3,K=6, ρ=3exp(−h/4)−2. The stability boundary is h=4ln3≈4.39445 s. At h=3 s, ρ≈−0.58290034 and the sampled controller is stable. At h=5 s it is≈−1.14048561 and is unstable in the unlimited model. The continuous proportional controller remains stable for both choices because it has no sample period.

Forward Euler applied to the continuous closed-loop differential equation gives a different multiplier ρ_E=1−h(G+K)/C. At h=3 for the example, ρ_E=−1.25, so that numerical approximation is unstable although the exact sampled controller above is stable. An exact sample-and-hold update and an approximate integrator answer different questions. For G=0 their multipliers happen to coincide in this scalar example; that does not identify the two interpretations generally. Read [A tank balance explains control offset, delay and saturation](../../utilities/water-and-sanitation/operations/A-tank-balance-explains-control-offset-delay-and-saturation.lingua#r_2R6H6TT78N3TG1PYZMNFJ14RYB) with this distinction explicit.

Saturation changes the held-command map again. For the nominal C=12,G=3,K=6,r=2,u₀=6,d=b=0 model with bounds 0≤u≤20 and h=8, let a=exp(−2). There is an exact two-cycle L_c=6a/(1+3a)≈0.577530812 K, H_c=6/(1+3a)≈4.267407565 K. At H_c≥3 the command clips to zero, giving the next state aH_c=L_c. At L_c the command 18−6L_c is admissible, giving next state 6(1−a)+(3a−2)L_c=H_c. These conditions hold because 0<a<1/3. The two-step local derivative a(3a−2) has magnitude less than one, so this cycle is locally attracting within these active regions. Bounded actuation has prevented unlimited growth but has not restored tracking of 2 K.

**Read and work:** [Feedback Control Systems MIT 16 30 digital control](../sources/Feedback-Control-Systems-MIT-16-30-digital-control.lingua#r_002CEE25F9B16E0755767D7EFD), Topic 20's sampled measurement and zero-order-hold passages. Derive the exact recurrence using the existing stored-energy solution, then derive the Euler approximation separately. State which effects remain excluded by both. Prove the two-cycle algebra and region conditions before comparing the program's two-step trajectory. Connect computation deadlines to [Separate safety from progress and account for waiting](../../computer-science/core/Separate-safety-from-progress-and-account-for-waiting.lingua#r_00BBBFDC1DF17364A8D5372BB8): eventual completion is not a bound on when a new control command becomes available.

